Load test setup
---------------

1. Grinder
The assumption is that the you execute the start scripts in the logon-test-load directory. The scripts are not yet CWD aware.

1.1 Running grinder
- start the grinder console
  bin/start_grinder_console.sh
- start a grinder agent (on the same machine)
  bin/start_agent.sh

  Now you are able to run the configured grinder script (grinder.properties:grinder.script). Through the console you see what happens.
  The agent creates the following two directories as output:
  <agent starting directory>/log                     --> contains the out and data files of grinder
  <agent starting directory>/<agent name>-file-store --> the staging directory of the agent

1.2 Record a test
- start the tcpproxy
  * bin/start_proxy.sh -console -http > <script name>
  This will open the TCPProxy console, with which you can add comments between the recorded requests.
  Change the browser proxy configuration to use the TCPProxy from grinder (Checkout FoxyProxy for simpler proxy handling)
  Make sure to delete browser cache and logins (FF shortcut, ctrl-shift-delete)
  
- post modification
  A) To import the global urls used for testing add the following line as import to the recorded script:
    * from scripts.base_config import url0
    --> This will set the configured url0 value in the base_config
    * remove the line with url0 value
  B) To give each script a unique range of test numbers edit the resulting scripts and replace
    * from net.grinder.script import Test
    with
    from net.grinder.script import Test as StandardTest
    def Test(number, description):
       # Adjust the 10000 to the appropriate offest
       return StandardTest(number+ 10000, description)
    
- SSL recording does not work for the https://logonebm2-int.zh.adnovum.ch because there is a problem with the ca.
  For recording the proxy opens two SSL connections [browser -- proxy -- logon] for both connections the proxy
  uses its own certificates, which is a problem for the ebm2 environment.
  
  
Directory Structure
-------------------
The whole test-load directory is used to send to the grinder agent, so that everything is available.
|-- bin          --> grinder scripts (windows, linux)
|-- etc          --> grinder configuration
`-- scripts      --> basic scripts
    `-- usecases --> recorded usecases
    
How to run tests on logonebm2
-----------------------------
* make sure you have a /tmp/logon directory (for moving over the dist-load.zip file)
* login into the windows machine with rdesktop:
  rdesktop -g 1400x1050 -r disk:mcico=/tmp/logon logonebm2-int.zh.adnovum.ch -u Administrator -p <standard root pw>
  - the -r option maps your local /tmp/ on the logonebm2-int.zh.adnovum.ch, to copy over the load-test.zip
* create the dist-load zip file:
  adnant clean dist (execute in subfolder 'logon-test-load')
  cp build/dist/test-load.zip /tmp/logon
* extract the test-load.zip on the Desktop
* open a command prompt and cd into the extracted test-load zip file
* start the grinder console
  bin\start_console.cmd
* start the grinder agent (open another command prompt)
  bin\start_agent.cmd
* modify the etc/grinder.properties script to make sure the right script is executed
* redistribute the files to the agents (Skript -> "Send changes" button (most right one))
* modify scripts to use the right login, change the following values to the right user:
  - j_username
  - j_password
  
OpTrace Analysis
----------------
Use the jcan-optrace scripts on the application log file
jcan-optrace/etc/tools *

loadchart:
~/projects/jcan-optrace/dist/tools/loadchart.pl --name loadchart --period 30 server.*
bin size 30 min.

profile:
~/projects/jcan-optrace/dist/tools/profile.pl -threshold 100000 server.log > profile.html
only show requests longer than 1000 ms

peperino:
~/projects/jcan-optrace/dist/tools/paperino.pl -name paperino server.log

GroundReport Analysis
---------------------
- Install jython
- Install groundreport
- Setup a postgressql database (see the groundreport readme)
- configure ground report (see the readme)
- import the data by using the  ./databaseInterface.bash script
  * make sure you have configured the right data files (etc/data.properties)
  * select option 4 to upload a file
- check the loadrun information (remember the run version if there are more than one)
- generate the report
  * change the etc/groundReport.properties to use the right run
  * run the ./generateReport.bash script
- Find the freshly generated reports in the output directory

GrinderAnalyzer
--------------
- Install jython
- Install GrinderAnalyzer
- jython ./analyzer.py <path to the grinder data file> <path to the grinder out file>
- find the reports in the grinderReports directory

FAQ
---
1. What is the __init__.py script for?
   It defines a python module so that the import is possible in sub directories
